(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-prof-shah-style-quiz-version-3"]={"title":"Prof. Shah Style Integration Set 3 — Anatomy Exam 4","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"Which broad superficial back muscle extends, adducts, and medially rotates the arm at the shoulder?","choices":["Deltoid","Latissimus dorsi","Supraspinatus","Infraspinatus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Latissimus dorsi is the broad superficial muscle producing extension, adduction, and medial rotation at the shoulder. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which rotator cuff muscle occupies the supraspinous fossa and ABDUCTS the arm at the shoulder?","choices":["Infraspinatus","Teres minor","Supraspinatus","Subscapularis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Supraspinatus sits in the supraspinous fossa and abducts the arm as part of the rotator cuff. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which rotator cuff muscle lies in the subscapular fossa on the anterior scapula and MEDIALLY rotates the arm?","choices":["Supraspinatus","Subscapularis","Infraspinatus","Teres minor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Subscapularis occupies the subscapular fossa (anterior scapula) and medially rotates the arm. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which muscle arises from the coracoid process of the scapula and adducts the arm at the shoulder?","choices":["Brachialis","Biceps brachii","Deltoid","Coracobrachialis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Coracobrachialis arises from the coracoid process and adducts the arm at the shoulder. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which muscle is a PURE elbow flexor in the anterior compartment of the arm (it only flexes the elbow)?","choices":["Biceps brachii","Triceps brachii","Brachioradialis","Brachialis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Brachialis is the pure elbow flexor of the anterior arm compartment. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which anterior superficial forearm muscle pronates the forearm and helps flex the elbow?","choices":["Pronator teres","Supinator","Pronator quadratus","Brachioradialis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pronator teres, in the anterior superficial forearm, pronates the forearm (and assists elbow flexion). — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which muscle adducts the scapula and produces downward rotation of the scapula?","choices":["Serratus anterior","Rhomboid major","Pectoralis minor","Levator scapulae"],"answer":1,"exp":"The rhomboids (major and minor) adduct the scapula and rotate it downward. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which muscle, as its name implies, ELEVATES the scapula?","choices":["Serratus anterior","Subclavius","Rhomboid major","Levator scapulae"],"answer":3,"exp":"Levator scapulae elevates the scapula. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which facial muscle elevates the corner of the mouth (as in smiling) and is innervated by the facial nerve (CN VII)?","choices":["Depressor anguli oris","Zygomaticus major","Mentalis","Buccinator"],"answer":1,"exp":"Zygomaticus major elevates the corner of the mouth (smiling) and, like all facial-expression muscles, is innervated by CN VII. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which extra-ocular muscle ADDUCTS the eye (rotates it medially) and is innervated by cranial nerve III?","choices":["Lateral rectus","Medial rectus","Superior oblique","Superior rectus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The medial rectus rotates the eye medially (adduction) and, like most extra-ocular muscles, is supplied by CN III. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which muscle of mastication acts on the internal surface of the mandibular ramus, elevates the jaw, and is innervated by the mandibular division of CN V?","choices":["Masseter","Temporalis","Medial pterygoid","Lateral pterygoid"],"answer":2,"exp":"The medial pterygoid acts on the internal (medial) surface of the ramus, elevates the jaw, and is supplied by the mandibular nerve (V3). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which tongue muscle depresses the tongue (moving it in and down) and is innervated by the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)?","choices":["Palatoglossus","Genioglossus","Hyoglossus","Styloglossus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hyoglossus draws the tongue in and down (depression) and is supplied by CN XII. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which superficial intrinsic back muscle runs from the cervical spinous processes to the mastoid process and extends/rotates the head?","choices":["Spinalis","Longissimus","Multifidus","Splenius capitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Splenius capitis is a superficial intrinsic back muscle running from the cervical spinous processes to the mastoid process, extending and rotating the head. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which erector spinae column is the INTERMEDIATE (middle) of the three and extends the vertebral column?","choices":["Longissimus","Spinalis","Iliocostalis","Quadratus lumborum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Longissimus is the intermediate column of the erector spinae (between spinalis medially and iliocostalis laterally). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which muscle DEPRESSES the ribs (expiration), running from the superior border of each rib to the inferior border of the rib above?","choices":["External intercostal","Diaphragm","Scalenes","Internal intercostal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Internal intercostals run from the superior border of a rib to the inferior border of the rib above and depress the ribs (expiration). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which anterior beak-like projection of the scapula serves as an attachment for the coracobrachialis, pectoralis minor, and the short head of the biceps?","choices":["Coracoid process","Acromion","Glenoid cavity","Coronoid process"],"answer":0,"exp":"The coracoid process is the anterior hook-like projection of the scapula and an attachment point for the coracobrachialis, pectoralis minor, and biceps short head. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which rounded structure on the LATERAL distal humerus articulates with the head of the radius?","choices":["Trochlea","Trochlear notch","Capitulum","Medial epicondyle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The capitulum is the rounded lateral condyle of the distal humerus that articulates with the head of the radius. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which spool-shaped MEDIAL structure of the distal humerus articulates with the trochlear notch of the ulna?","choices":["Trochlea","Capitulum","Coronoid fossa","Greater tubercle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The trochlea is the spool-shaped medial condyle of the humerus that fits the trochlear notch of the ulna. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which groove on the proximal humerus lies BETWEEN the greater and lesser tubercles?","choices":["Radial groove","Olecranon fossa","Anatomical neck","Intertubercular sulcus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The intertubercular sulcus (bicipital groove) lies between the greater and lesser tubercles of the humerus. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which proximal radial landmark is the site where the biceps brachii tendon attaches?","choices":["Radial tuberosity","Radial styloid process","Ulnar notch of the radius","Olecranon"],"answer":0,"exp":"The radial tuberosity, just distal to the neck of the radius, is where the biceps brachii tendon inserts. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which large projection on the LATERAL proximal femur is a major muscle-attachment landmark you can palpate at the hip?","choices":["Greater trochanter","Lesser trochanter","Fovea","Intercondylar fossa"],"answer":0,"exp":"The greater trochanter is the large lateral projection of the proximal femur and a major muscle-attachment site. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which distal landmark is formed by the FIBULA and is felt as the lateral ankle bump?","choices":["Medial malleolus","Lateral malleolus","Tibial tuberosity","Head of the fibula"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lateral malleolus is the distal end of the fibula — the lateral ankle prominence. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which bone contains the mastoid process, the styloid process, and the external acoustic meatus?","choices":["Occipital bone","Parietal bone","Sphenoid","Temporal bone"],"answer":3,"exp":"The temporal bone bears the mastoid process, styloid process, external acoustic meatus, and the zygomatic process. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which opening transmits the optic nerve (CN II) and the ophthalmic artery?","choices":["Superior orbital fissure","Foramen ovale","Optic canal","Foramen magnum"],"answer":2,"exp":"The optic canal transmits the optic nerve (II) and the ophthalmic artery. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which opening transmits the glossopharyngeal (IX), vagus (X), and accessory (XI) nerves?","choices":["Jugular foramen","Stylomastoid foramen","Optic canal","Foramen magnum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The jugular foramen (between the temporal and occipital bones) transmits CN IX, X, and XI. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which opening transmits the facial nerve (CN VII)?","choices":["Foramen ovale","Jugular foramen","Stylomastoid foramen","Optic canal"],"answer":2,"exp":"The stylomastoid foramen (temporal bone) transmits the facial nerve, CN VII. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which cranial suture lies between the frontal bone and the two parietal bones?","choices":["Sagittal suture","Lambdoid suture","Coronal suture","Squamous suture"],"answer":2,"exp":"The coronal suture is between the frontal bone and the two parietal bones. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which vertebra has a long, prominent spinous process and gives origin to the ligamentum nuchae?","choices":["Atlas (C1)","C7 (vertebra prominens)","Axis (C2)","Thoracic vertebra (T6)"],"answer":1,"exp":"C7, the vertebra prominens, has a long prominent spinous process where the ligamentum nuchae begins (extending to the external occipital crest). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which part of the sternum is the inferior tip, which ossifies late and serves as a landmark for CPR hand placement?","choices":["Xiphoid process","Manubrium","Body","Costal cartilage"],"answer":0,"exp":"The xiphoid process is the small inferior tip of the sternum. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which pharyngeal muscle group constricts the pharynx during swallowing and is innervated by the vagus nerve (CN X)?","choices":["Laryngeal elevators","Pharyngeal constrictors","Palatal muscles","Scalenes"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pharyngeal constrictors (superior, middle, inferior) squeeze the pharynx during swallowing and are innervated by CN X. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"}]};
